WEBVTT

00:00:07.705 --> 00:00:11.378
A registration list contains names with extra spaces,

00:00:11.378 --> 00:00:13.388
a short name, and empty text.

00:00:13.388 --> 00:00:16.645
We will clean the text before choosing names by

00:00:16.645 --> 00:00:17.130
length.

00:00:33.036 --> 00:00:36.547
A stream pipeline describes a source and a sequence of

00:00:36.547 --> 00:00:38.302
operations on its elements.

00:00:38.302 --> 00:00:39.602
Our source is names.

00:00:39.602 --> 00:00:42.398
Map and filter are intermediate operations.

00:00:42.398 --> 00:00:44.673
Each produces another stream stage.

00:00:44.673 --> 00:00:48.119
Mapping applies a function to each processed element.

00:00:48.119 --> 00:00:50.264
Here, trim supplies cleaned text.

00:00:50.264 --> 00:00:53.775
Filter uses a lambda returning true or false to decide

00:00:53.775 --> 00:00:55.725
which cleaned values continue.

00:00:55.725 --> 00:00:58.911
These operations leave the source list unchanged.

00:00:59.209 --> 00:01:02.563
A terminal operation starts processing and produces

00:01:02.563 --> 00:01:03.680
the final result.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:06.705
Here, toList collects results into a List, the

00:01:06.705 --> 00:01:09.467
interface type of the returned collection.

00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:13.018
The result is unmodifiable, meaning its entries cannot

00:01:13.018 --> 00:01:15.056
be added, removed, or replaced.

00:01:15.056 --> 00:01:18.344
Encounter order is the order supplied by a source.

00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:21.040
This pipeline preserves our list's order.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:24.525
A HashSet source would not gain a promised order just

00:01:24.525 --> 00:01:25.709
by using a stream.

00:01:25.814 --> 00:01:28.869
Predict every output line, including the count.

00:01:28.869 --> 00:01:32.314
Apply trimming before the length test, then trace the

00:01:32.314 --> 00:01:33.549
loop over selected.

00:01:33.549 --> 00:01:34.264
Pause here.

00:01:39.091 --> 00:01:42.135
Maya and Luis each have four characters after

00:01:42.135 --> 00:01:43.826
trimming, so they remain.

00:01:43.826 --> 00:01:47.006
Li has two and empty text has zero, so they are

00:01:47.006 --> 00:01:47.615
excluded.

00:01:47.615 --> 00:01:50.659
The loop prints Maya before Luis, followed by

00:01:50.659 --> 00:01:51.606
Selected: two.

00:01:51.606 --> 00:01:55.191
Filter keeps every matching entry, including repeats.

00:01:55.547 --> 00:01:58.914
Add another Maya with surrounding spaces at the end of

00:01:58.914 --> 00:01:59.912
the source list.

00:01:59.912 --> 00:02:03.217
Predict every output line and the new count, then run

00:02:03.217 --> 00:02:03.591
again.

00:02:03.591 --> 00:02:06.522
Where should the repeated name appear, and why?
